  "worked_solution": "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=0,$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=0=f(0)\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is not right-continuous at $x=0,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=1\\ne 0=f(0)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=0,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true.",
